From 621fb3b156f24728e0b2ff649f1e140832632933 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Viet Hung Nguyen <hvn@calif.io>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 17:18:51 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Add allocation-reusing value decoding

The generic `Reader` builds a fresh `Value` tree for every datum. Each
record allocates a new vector, clones every field name, and recursively
allocates its allocation-bearing children, so a caller decoding many
records of the same shape pays for that shape over and over. In an
allocation-heavy fixture, allocator and free functions accounted for about
34% of exclusive sampled CPU time.

Add a decoding path that writes into caller-owned storage and reuses what
is already there:

  Reader::read_into(&mut Value) -> AvroResult<bool>
  GenericDatumReader::read_value_into(&mut R, &mut Value)
  OwnedGenericDatumReader, an owning variant of GenericDatumReader that
  resolves schema references once, for use in a long-lived reader

Records, arrays, unions, strings, bytes, fixed values, and enums reuse
compatible existing allocations. Maps, `Decimal`, `BigDecimal`, and `Uuid`
own representations with no public in-place decode API and fall back to
`decode_internal`, as does reader-schema resolution, which consumes a
`Value` and can change its shape.

This is one decode path, not two. `Block::read_next`,
`GenericDatumReader::read_value`, and `OwnedGenericDatumReader::read_value`
delegate to the `_into` forms with a fresh `Value::Null`, which finds
nothing to reuse and so costs what it always did. That keeps block
bookkeeping and error behavior from drifting between the two forms, and it
means the existing test suite exercises the new decoder.

The new methods are additive: no existing signature or behavior changes.

Measured on two workloads:

  Eight levels of nested records, strings, byte arrays, arrays, and
  unions: 553,483 -> 1,699,095 records/s (3.07x), with allocation and
  reallocation operations falling from 1,560,003 to 81.

  A 2,000-entry, 20-column Iceberg v2 manifest, where each metrics map is
  an `array<record{key,value}>`: 85.5k -> 388.2k entries/s (4.51x), with
  allocation operations for the whole file falling from 902,003 to 455.

The operations that remain are first-tree and block-buffer setup rather
than per-datum work.
